.TH CCOL_R_MEMPOOL_USED_COUNT 3 "2026-09-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_r_mempool_used_count \- get number of used entries for a size
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

size_t ccol_r_mempool_used_count(ccol_r_mempool *rmp, size_t size);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns how many entries are allocated from the pool that would serve the
given size, not counting any dynamic allocation.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
rmp   Ranged memory pool to query
size  Size in bytes to query (maps to a specific sub-pool)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns number of entries in use for this size
Returns 0 on error
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
It returns 0 if size is invalid, which means 0 or above largest_size
It rounds size up to the next power-of-two pool
Thread-safe if the pool was created with single_threaded=false
The pool tracks a dynamic allocation separately

The count is exact whenever the sub-pool is quiet. An entry parked in the
cache of a thread counts as free, because nothing holds it. Under
concurrent allocation on a tier that keeps a cache for each thread, the
result can sit a few entries above ccol_r_mempool_total_capacity() for
that size for a short time, but it never goes above the physical slot
count that ccol_r_mempool_allocated_bytes(3) accounts for. See
ccol_mempool_total_capacity(3) for the reason.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_r_mempool_total_capacity (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count (3)
